sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design (Nick 2026-11-07): task VMs are fixed, reused slots named <node>-tvm-<slot>. Each slot is a permanent Orbit Node whose identity is written once, here. A claim (docs/cli/tasks/provision.mmd) only starts it; a release (docs/cli/tasks/vm-slot-release.mmd) rolls it back to `clean`. Decided by the orchestrator from that end state: the identity lives on a small volume per slot on the host, so a slot keeps it when it is rebuilt from a new image.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · enrol or re-enrol one slot
  %% kind: task_vm.slot_enrol
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane HUB icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=slot tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>Incus over ssh
  participant HUB as VPN hub<br/>over ssh
  participant VM as Slot<br/>shark-tvm-3
  Note over GW: Record the slot<br/>once per slot number
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - the `slots` count of a task VM host (`node:update`): one enrol per missing slot, one at a time
  %%> - the image refresh: it re-enrols the first idle slot from the candidate, then each other idle slot after the promotion, one at a time
  %%> - a release, when the slot's image is no longer the host's promoted image
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - an active task VM host, and no group holds the slot
  %%> Result on the first enrol only:
  %%> - the Node `shark-tvm-3` with its `app-dev` role, WireGuard address, agent secret, the `pi-server` Process with its Pi token, and the workspace Instance at the fixed checkout path
  %%> - the agent secret and the Pi token go to the vault; the run inputs hold only their key names
  %%> A re-enrol writes no record: the slot keeps all of them
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-vm-slot-enrol
  SW->>SW: Issue the model key<br/>workflow · task-vm-model-issue
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - makes the slot's model key and registers it with the model router; the key stays in the run and goes only into the identity volume
  %%> Guard:
  %%> - skipped when the slot has its model key; a key is revoked only when the slot is retired
  SW->>H: Write the identity<br/>ansible phase · identity
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - creates the volume `shark-tvm-3-identity` once
  %%> - makes the WireGuard key pair and the SSH host keys in the volume, and writes the agent secret, the Pi token, the model key, the hostname and the WireGuard address
  %%> - prints only the WireGuard public key and the host key fingerprint
  %%> Fixed:
  %%> - a key or secret that exists is never made again, so every rebuild keeps the slot's identity; a private key never leaves the host
  SW->>HUB: Add the hub peer<br/>peer file · once
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the slot's peer file from the public key above and reloads the hub
  %%> Guard:
  %%> - skipped when the peer file already matches; a claim or a release never touches the hub
  SW->>H: Rebuild the slot VM<br/>ansible phase · slot
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - deletes the slot's VM and its seed clone when they exist
  %%> - clones the seed of the host's promoted image (the candidate, for the refresh), and creates `shark-tvm-3` from that image with the seed clone, the identity volume, its fixed address and MAC
  %%> - starts it; the image's identity unit installs the identity before sshd, WireGuard, the agent and Pi start
  SW->>VM: Check ready<br/>one exec
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the same check as a claim: the agent, Pi, the tunnel and the three containers; Swamp's `assert` decides
  SW->>H: Take the clean snapshot<br/>stop · VM and seed clone
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - stops the slot, then snapshots the VM and its seed clone as `clean`; a release rolls back to it
  %%> - a stopped slot holds disk, not memory
  alt the run passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>public key · image
    Note over GW: Record the slot free<br/>with its image
    %%> Result:
    %%> - records the WireGuard public key and host key on the first enrol, and the image fingerprint on every enrol
    %%> - the slot is `free`
  else a step failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - the slot is `failed` and no claim takes it; the identity volume and the hub peer stay
    %%> - running the enrol again is safe: it keeps the keys and rebuilds the VM
  end
